## 7. npm dependencies

Read from the `license` field that npm records for every package in the two
checked-in npm lockfiles, deduplicated on name + version + licence.
Quatricmorph's own workspace packages are excluded (they appear as
`"link": true` entries).

| Lockfile | Version | Unique third-party packages | Role |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `apps/web/package-lock.json` | 3 | 128 | the set CI installs — `.github/workflows/build.yaml` runs `npm install` in `apps/web` |
| `apps/web/quatricmorph-workspace/package-lock.json` | 3 | 169 | a **divergent** lockfile, audited here for completeness (see below) |
| union, deduplicated | — | **203** | the table below |

Only three name+version pairs across both lockfiles are runtime dependencies of
a shipped product, and all three are MIT: `three` 0.185.1, `lil-gui` 0.19.2
(what `apps/web/package-lock.json` resolves for
`apps/web/quatricmorph-workspace/package.json`), and `lil-gui` 0.21.0 (what the
divergent nested lockfile resolves). Every other entry in the table is
`"dev": true` — TypeScript, Vite, Vitest and their transitive tooling.

**The nested lockfile does not match its own `package.json`.**
`apps/web/quatricmorph-workspace/package.json` declares
`"name": "quatricmorph-workspace"`, `"version": "0.1.0"`, `lil-gui ^0.19.2`,
`vite ^7.1.14`, `vitest ^4.1.10`. The root entry of
`apps/web/quatricmorph-workspace/package-lock.json` declares
`"name": "quatricmorph"`, `"version": "0.0.0"`, `lil-gui ^0.21.0`,
`vite ^8.2.0`, `typescript ~7.0.2`, `vitest ^3.2.7`. It is therefore stale
relative to the manifest beside it. Its packages are included in the table
because it is checked in and its licence metadata is readable; they are **not**
the set any documented command installs.

A **third** lockfile, `apps/web/quatricmorph-workspace/bun.lock`, also names the
package `quatricmorph` and pins `lil-gui ^0.21.0`, `three ^0.185.1`,
`@types/three ^0.185.3`, `typescript ~7.0.2`, `vite ^8.2.0`, `vitest ^4.1.10`.
**Not verified**: bun's lockfile format carries no per-package `license` field
(and is not strict JSON — `jq` rejects it on trailing commas), so no licence can
be read from it. It is not the dependency set CI installs.

Noted, not fatal: the twelve `lightningcss*` packages at 1.33.0 are **MPL-2.0**,
a file-level (weak) copyleft licence. They are `"dev": true` — optional platform
binaries in Vite's toolchain — and no MPL-2.0 file is redistributed in any build
artifact this repository produces.

